Add terminal.text_mode, to leave the VT's mode to the sessions

greetd sets the VT to text mode before every session, so that textual
sessions show. A graphical session, or a graphical greeter, that takes over
from a boot splash then starts on the text console: the splash's last frame
goes, and the console shows until the session's compositor takes the VT.

With text_mode = false in [terminal], greetd leaves the VT in the mode it
finds it in. It still clears the VT, which changes nothing on screen while
the VT is in graphics mode. The default, true, keeps greetd's behaviour.

greetd now also resets the VT when the greeter exits without starting a
session, or a later session or greeter fails to start, as it already did
when the first one failed to start. With text_mode = false, a greeter that
died before its compositor took the VT would otherwise leave the splash's
last frame on screen, and nothing of why.

The reset also sets the VT's keyboard to Unicode mode. A previous user of
the VT can leave its keyboard off (K_OFF): Omarchy's boot splash does, as
its seatd, still rooted in the initramfs, most likely cannot reopen the VT
to restore it. Only logind restores the keyboard, when a compositor that
took the VT through it lets go, so a greeter that never took the VT left a
console that took no keys, VT switches included. A keyboard mode that
cannot be set is logged, and the reset carries on.

